  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes key design challenges and strategies for a membrane placed between a cathode and an anode, likely in an alkaline alcohol fuel-cell-type system. At the center is a membrane layer sandwiched between catalyst-coated electrodes, with the cathode side shown in pink and the anode side in blue. Arrows connect this membrane to enlarged sketches that highlight two main themes: chemical stability and mechanical stability.\n\nOn the chemical side, one zoom-in shows hydroxide ions interacting with positively charged cation groups attached to the polymer network, illustrating vulnerability to OH⁻ attack and the need for chemically robust ion-conducting groups. Another zoom-in addresses alcohol solubility, showing ethanol molecules approaching or entering the membrane, suggesting that limiting alcohol uptake or crossover is important.\n\nOn the mechanical side, two different stabilization approaches are depicted. One uses crosslinking, where polymer chains are tied together by many small linking points to strengthen the network. The other uses reinforcement, represented by an interwoven fibrous scaffold that appears to improve structural durability. Overall, the image conveys that an effective membrane must simultaneously resist chemical degradation, control alcohol transport, and maintain mechanical integrity.",
